<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · sea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sea of Azov</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Sea of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azov">Azov</a> is a shallow inland sea north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>, linked to it by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerch-strait">Kerch Strait</a> and fed by the Don and Kuban rivers. Its shores and the surrounding steppe were home to successive nomadic peoples, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgars">Bulgar</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/onogurs">Onogurs</a> and Utigurs, and were later contested by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazars</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. In 1919 Makhno's anarchist Free Territory reached its northern coast.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sea-of-azov.jpg" alt="Sea of Azov" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Канопус Киля · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Azow_Sea_Sunset.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Sea of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azov">Azov</a> is a shelf sea about 360 kilometres long and 180 kilometres wide, with an area of 39,000 square kilometres. It joins the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> through the narrow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerch-strait">Strait of Kerch</a>, about 4 kilometres wide, and is sometimes regarded as a northern extension of the Black Sea. The Don and Kuban rivers bring sand, silt and shells, which build bays, limans and long narrow spits; the Arabat Spit stretches over 112 kilometres. The inflow keeps the salinity low, almost fresh in places, and the sea is the shallowest in the world, between 0.9 and 14 metres deep. The name Azov probably derives from a settlement whose name comes from the Kipchak Turkish asak or azaq, meaning lowlands.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Traces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> settlement lie in the area now covered by the sea. In antiquity it was known as the Maeotis Swamp, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maeotians">Maeotians</a> who lived by fishing and farming near its northeastern marshes; it remains unclear whether the sea took its name from them or they from it. The marshes around the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/don-river">Tanais</a>, today's Don, served as a check on the migration of nomadic peoples from the steppe. Ancient geographers had only a vague idea of its extent: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> judged it as large as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>, Pseudo-Scylax about half as large, and it was long believed to connect directly with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arctic-ocean">Arctic Ocean</a>.</p><p>The sea was often a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> of conflict between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, seeking naval expansion to the south, and the Ottoman power. In 1695–96 Peter I led two campaigns to take the Turkish fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azov">Azov</a>, defended by a garrison of 7,000. The first, in 1695, brought an army of 31 thousand men and 170 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a> to the fortress; after two failed assaults on 5 August and 25 September the siege was lifted. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimean-war">Crimean War</a>, British and French forces took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerch">Kerch</a> on 12 May 1855 and then besieged Taganrog, which withstood them despite their great superiority in numbers, and their army withdrew around August 1855.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The sea has long been an important waterway, and navigation grew after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a>–Don Canal was built in 1952, linking it with the Volga. The major ports are Taganrog, Mariupol, Yeysk and Berdyansk. Fishing has long been a major activity, with sturgeon, pike-perch and bream among the valuable species. Dams, over-fishing and pollution have reduced the catch, and the anchovy fisheries have collapsed.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sea of Azov” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Greek settlers, mostly from Miletus, founded towns on both shores of the Cimmerian Bosporus, the Kerch Strait linking the Black Sea with the <b>Sea of Azov</b>, from the early sixth century BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bosporan-kingdom">Bosporan Kingdom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>As the Revolutionary Insurgent Army of Ukraine grew, it controlled a large area between the Dnieper and the <b>Sea of Azov</b>, which anarchists called the Free Territory.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-territory">Free Territory</a></li><li><blockquote>They settled in the steppe between the Kuban, the <b>Sea of Azov</b> and the lower Don, in contact with the Caucasus and Byzantine Crimea.</blockquote><a href="/state/onogurs">Onogurs</a></li><li><blockquote>The Utigurs appear in Byzantine sources in the early 6th century as one of the two main Bulgar or Hunnic groups of the Pontic steppe, living east of the Don and around the <b>Sea of Azov</b>, with the related Kutrigurs to the west.</blockquote><a href="/state/utigurs">Utigurs</a></li></ul></section>
